{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>Did you offer</b> — According to the plain sense, the heh of הזבחים is the heh of the interrogative, and the meaning is that they did not bring. Our predecessors of blessed memory said: the Levites brought them. And if it is a tradition, we have received it, for according to the plain sense they did not bring them. And the one who explains that one lamb in the morning and one in the evening would not amount to a perutah for any one of you — he has no reason, for HaShem did not command to bring more, and behold Israel did offer the daily burnt offering at Mount Sinai for fifty days, and Israel was in a settled state, for on the first of Nisan in the second year the Tabernacle was erected, and in the second month on the twentieth of the month they journeyed from Sinai — fifty days. And all the time they were at Sinai they were in a settled state. And they had wine when they stood in the wilderness of Sinai for about a year, and they had libation wine, and afterward they journeyed and carried wine with them, until the Day of Atonement would pass, for so it is written in the section of Aḥarei Mot, 'and he did as YHWH commanded Moses.' And they came to the land of Arabah and the ravine, and there was no pasture for their flocks and they had no wine — only in the fortieth year did they plunder the Midianites and Amorites. And likewise when they came to Kadesh, in a settled state, in the fortieth year they plundered the Midianites and acquired livestock. And behold, by way of reasoning they did not bring offerings, and there is no argument from the reckoning of forty days, for one day in a year counts as a year. And the meaning is: 'When I led you in the wilderness for many years, you did not bring Me burnt offerings,' rather I commanded you to do justice — and likewise, 'For I desire kindness and not sacrifice.' And so in Jeremiah He says: 'For I did not speak to your fathers nor command them concerning matters of burnt offering and sacrifice.'" "<b>And it shall pour out like water</b> — that it shall not cease, in the manner of 'your peace shall be like a river.'"], :book :amos, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {0 "The comment addresses Amos 5:25, where the Hebrew reads הַזֶּבַח וּמִנְחָה הִקְרַבְתֶּם לִי; Ibn Ezra reads the prefixed heh on הזבחים as interrogative. The final citations are from Hosea 6:6 and Jeremiah 7:22. The phrase ואין טענה מחשבון ארבעים יום likely alludes to a dispute over the forty-day periods Moses spent on Sinai; translated hyper-literally.", 1 "The lemma and comparison are from Isaiah 48:18 (וְהָיָה כַנָּהָר שְׁלוֹמֵךְ)."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Amos -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/amos"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:18:23.796800119Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}